WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.827
Spark gives us more than one way to express structured data transformations.

00:00:04.827 --> 00:00:10.727
We can use Spark S Q L, which feels familiar if you already know S Q L.

00:00:10.727 --> 00:00:16.469
We can also use the data frame A P I, where transformations are written as code.

00:00:17.037 --> 00:00:19.959
These are not two completely separate worlds.

00:00:19.959 --> 00:00:25.444
Both can use Spark's structured processing engine.

00:00:25.444 --> 00:00:31.974
For this course, Spark S Q L is a good bridge because you can focus on concepts you already understand:

00:00:31.974 --> 00:00:40.239
selecting columns, filtering rows, joining data, grouping values, and using window functions.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:48.073
Then, when you see the same operation written with a data frame, you can connect the code back to the S Q L idea.
